From: gaokun-android contributors <nekovahiru@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5: heavily ratelimit the repeated handover error

q6v5_handover_interrupt() dev_err()s every time a handover is signalled after
the first one. On a Huawei MateBook E Go (SC8280XP) the SLPI signals it about
five times a second, forever, and it never stops:

  qcom_q6v5_pas 2400000.remoteproc: Handover signaled, but it already happened

/proc/interrupts shows why the message is harmless and also why it will not go
away: the "q6v5 ready" and "q6v5 handover" smp2p bits of this remote carry
*identical* counts and advance together -- 303 each over 60 seconds -- while
adsp and nsp0 sit at 2 apiece. The remote keeps toggling both bits. The
handler already does the right thing, returning early without touching the
proxy resources, so the only consequence is the log.

That consequence is not small. This log flood is what erased the useful part
of an efi_pstore capture on that machine: pstore keeps only the tail of the
kernel log, so of 45 records saved across two panics, fewer than ten held
anything but this one line. A benign, self-repeating condition must not be
able to push a panic backtrace out of the crash log.

Plain dev_err_ratelimited() is not enough here, and that was measured rather
than assumed: with the default 10-per-5s budget the print rate on this machine
fell from 5.05/s to 1.95/s. A 62% cut still leaves some 7000 lines an hour.
For a condition that is permanent rather than bursty, the useful information
is carried by the first message and everything after it costs forensic budget.

Use an explicit ratelimit state of one message per minute. An unhealthy remote
is still visible in dmesg, a recurrence after a remoteproc restart still shows
up, and the message can no longer drown out a backtrace. The state is shared
by all remotes deliberately: it caps the total rate, while still letting
whichever device hits the condition be the one that reports it.

--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/qcom_aoss.h>
+#include <linux/ratelimit.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/smem.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/smem_state.h>
 #include <linux/remoteproc.h>
@@ -162,10 +163,18 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_q6v5_wait_for_start);
 
 static irqreturn_t q6v5_handover_interrupt(int irq, void *data)
 {
+	/*
+	 * A remote can assert this several times a second indefinitely. One
+	 * report a minute is plenty to notice it, and keeps the flood from
+	 * pushing a backtrace out of a pstore crash dump.
+	 */
+	static DEFINE_RATELIMIT_STATE(handover_rs, 60 * HZ, 1);
 	struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5 = data;
 
 	if (q6v5->handover_issued) {
-		dev_err(q6v5->dev, "Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
+		if (__ratelimit(&handover_rs))
+			dev_err(q6v5->dev,
+				"Handover signaled, but it already happened\n");
 		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 	}
 
